+so a local run with a development token still shows them. One of the four ("Write the project
+README", already `Done`) ships archived, so a fresh checkout shows both sides of the archive: three
+tasks in `GET /tasks` and the fourth under `GET /tasks?archived=true`.
+
+## Archiving
+
+Finished work should leave the list without leaving the system. A task carries a nullable
+`archivedAt`: `null` is an active task, a timestamp is an archived one, recording when it was
+filed away.
+
+```http
+POST /tasks/{id}/archive      200, the task with archivedAt set
+POST /tasks/{id}/unarchive    200, the task with archivedAt back to null
+```
+
+Both take no request body and both are idempotent - archiving an already-archived task returns it
+with its **original** `archivedAt`, because the field says when the task was filed away, not when
+someone last pressed the button. As everywhere else on this API, a task owned by someone else, or
+one that does not exist, is answered with 404.
+
+`GET /tasks` leaves archived tasks out. That default is the feature; it is not something a caller
+has to remember to ask for. To see them, use `?archived=`:
+
+| `?archived=` | returns |
+| --- | --- |
+| *(omitted)* or `false` | active tasks only |
+| `true` | the archive only |
+| `all` | both, i.e. what `GET /tasks` returned before archiving existed |
+
+The value is case-insensitive, and anything else - including an empty `?archived=` - is a **400**,
+the same reading `?priority=` gives a value it does not recognise. The two filters combine:
+`?archived=true&priority=High` is the high-priority part of the archive.
+
+`GET /tasks/{id}` returns archived tasks as usual. Archiving hides a task from the list, not from
+a caller holding its id, so a saved link does not rot into a 404.
+
+Archive state is **not** part of the PUT body, and a PUT never changes it - `archivedAt` survives
+an update the way `createdAt` and the owner do. If it were a field on the whole-resource PUT, any
+client still sending bodies written before the field existed would silently un-archive every task
+it edited, and archiving would become as easy to undo by accident as `DELETE` is. It is also not a
+fourth `TaskState`: status says how far the work got, archiving says whether you still want to see
+it, and folding them together would make "archive this without calling it Done" impossible.
+
+`DELETE` is untouched and still destroys a task outright. Archiving is the reversible option, not a
+replacement.
+
+> **Contract change.** Every task response now carries an `archivedAt` field, and `GET /tasks` no
+> longer returns archived tasks by default. Nothing is archived unless a caller archives it, so
+> existing lists are unaffected until someone uses the feature - `?archived=all` is the way back to
+> the old listing.
 
